<<~ ahu #/entry>>

! 2026-09-18 — the fold gate that ate the VM

Three agent sessions ran in parallel on the Enyalios host (64 GB, 12 threads; the VM at the default 31 GiB RAM + 8 GiB swap): two Claude sessions and one Codex session. At 14:23 PDT the Windows resource monitor spiked — disk churn, CPU pinned — and stayed there until the operator ran `wsl --shutdown` at 14:28. No process died on its own.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/timeline>>

!! Timeline (PDT)

|! when |! what |
| 14:14 | Claude session `7bdba225` resumes three background agents: the ablation neutral-letter fix, the grammar follow-ups, the tree-sitter four-backtick fence fix. |
| 14:19–14:20 | The fence agent writes a new `src/scanner.c` and regenerates `src/parser.c` in `packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext`. |
| 14:21:07 | The fence agent runs `rm -f memetic.so && pnpm run gate:fold` — the package's pytest gate, which loads the freshly built parser. The call passes its 120 s timeout and moves to the background. No memory cap. |
| 14:23:28 | First `page allocation failure` in the kernel log, named against `Xwayland` — the process that failed to get memory, not the one holding it. |
| 14:23–14:28 | Fifteen such warnings. Free swap reads 0 kB. `active_anon` reads 26.6 GB. No `oom-kill`, no `Killed process`. |
| 14:28:49 | Operator shuts WSL down. |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reproduction>>

!! Reproduction

Same afternoon, from the same tree, under a cap:

```sh
cd packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext/host-py
ulimit -v 3000000; timeout 100 python -m pytest -q
```

`test_memetic_lsp.py::test_diagnostics_name_a_broken_span` grew to 2 993 724 kB resident in 27.9 s of wall clock and died with a segmentation fault at the cap. About 100 MB/s, which fills 40 GB in six or seven minutes — a fit for the timeline.

The fault sits in the regenerated parser with the new external scanner, fed a broken span. The likely shape: a scanner token that consumes nothing, so error recovery allocates without bound. A C parser under Python carries no heap limit of its own.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ruled-out>>

!! Ruled out

* ''The ablation scratch script'' (`try-discount.js`, also backgrounded at 14:22) — node caps its heap near 4 GB; it cannot account for 40 GB.
* ''The grammar follow-ups agent'' — reading files at the time.
* ''Claude session `6381e425`'' — idle since 14:08.
* ''The Codex session'' — running `git log` and `cat`.
* ''Disk space'' — 88 GB of 1 TB used. The churn came from swap on the virtual disk, never from a full disk.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/lessons>>

!! What changed because of it

# ''Cap every native parser run'': `ulimit -v 4000000; timeout 120 <cmd>`. A capped run segfaults and hands the agent a red test; an uncapped run hands the operator a frozen desktop. Standing rule, kept in the agent memory as CAP NATIVE PARSER RUNS.
# ''A restarted agent replays its last step.'' The three agents' worktrees under `~/.claude/jobs/86a17260/tmp/` survived the shutdown; resuming the fence agent without the cap would have run the same gate again.
# ''The kernel alone will not save the VM.'' Swap gave it minutes to thrash and it took them. `earlyoom` closes that gap; `.wslconfig` tightens the ceiling it works under. Both land in [[lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/setup]]; the reasoning sits in [[lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/memory-guard]].
# ''The first `.wslconfig` edit changed nothing.'' `memory=32GB` and `swap=8GB` restated the defaults the VM already ran under. A setting that matches the default reads as a change and acts as none.

<<~/ahu>>

